#!/usr/bin/env node
/**
 * Trust store for overlay stack manifests.
 *
 * An overlay manifest (project .claude/stacks, user stacks directory, or a discovered plugin's
 * stack.json) carries gate commands that run on the user's machine, so it runs only after the user
 * approves its exact content. Approval is the sha256 of the canonical manifest text, stored in
 * <home>/.claude/sdd-kit/trusted-stacks.json, where <home> is the user's home directory from the OS user
 * database, never $HOME or CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR. Any edit to the manifest changes the hash and asks again.
 * Workflows never write this file; only `--approve` does.
 *
 * Usage:
 *   trust-store.mjs --list-untrusted [--json]   overlay manifests that are not approved
 *   trust-store.mjs --list [--json]             every overlay manifest with its status (trusted, untrusted or rejected)
 *   trust-store.mjs --show <source>             command-bearing fields of one manifest
 *   trust-store.mjs --approve <source>          record the manifest's current hash
 *   trust-store.mjs --hash <file>               print the canonical sha256 of a manifest file
 *   trust-store.mjs --plugin-dirs [--json]      add-ons this session loaded with --plugin-dir, plus .claude/stack-addons/*
 *   trust-store.mjs check <file>                exit 0 when the manifest is trusted, 1 when not, 2 on usage errors
 *                                               or when the workflows would reject or ignore the manifest
 *   trust-store.mjs --read-rules [--json]       whether the user settings hold the Read rules Detect needs
 *   trust-store.mjs --add-read-rules            add the missing ones to the user settings (only after the user agrees)
 *
 * --approve and check run the workflows' own manifest checks (mergeOverlay from the registry runtime), so
 * a manifest the workflows would reject or ignore is never approved or reported trusted (exit 2).
 *
 * The script is self-contained: tools/gen-stacks.mjs bundles the registry runtime and the first-party
 * entries into the generated region below, and the kit hash in the verification-gates skill covers this one
 * file. It loads no other script or registry file at run time, so a project copy that matches the hash
 * decides trust exactly as the shipped one does, whatever else the project plants beside it.
 *
 * Sources use the labels the workflows report: project:.claude/stacks/<file>,
 * user:~/.claude/stacks/<file>, plugin:<name>@<marketplace> and plugin:<name>@inline (an add-on
 * loaded with --plugin-dir: a directory under the project's .claude/stack-addons/, or a --plugin-dir
 * path on the Claude Code command line). --show and --approve also accept the short forms
 * user:stacks/<file>, plugin:<name> and project:.claude/stack-addons/<dir>/stack.json.
 */
